ord Bridge Toll House, on Victoria Embankment. Today it houses Bridge Sandwich Bar, a café. The building was built in 1870, according to Historic England (via Wikipedia). It is Grade II listed.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.15414,52.93832]},"69731":{"h":5.5,"base":4,"osm":"way/78046105","kind":"bridge","qid":"Q13528034","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Statue%20of%20Sir%20Robert%20Juckes%20Clifton%2C%20Wilford%20Bridge%2C%20Nottingham%20-%20Geograph-753649.jpg","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilford_Toll_Bridge","cat":"Wilford Toll Bridge","stories":[{"layer":"wilford","text":"The Wilford Village and Wilford Lane stops, on line 2 from the city centre to Clifton, opened in August 2015.","source":{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilford","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"2015","whenSrc":{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilford","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Trams to Wilford, 2015"},{"layer":"wilford","text":"Coal was found on the Clifton estate, and work on Clifton Colliery began in 1868. Sir Robert Juckes Clifton, the last of the Clifton baronets, died of typhoid in 1869; the colliery and this toll bridge, both his projects, opened in 1870.","source":{"title":"Robert Juckes Clifton","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Juckes_Clifton","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Sir Robert Clifton's colliery","see":[["Clifton Hall","35431"]]},{"layer":"wilford","text":"For some 400 years a ferry crossed the Trent here, until a temporary wooden footbridge replaced it in 1864. On 16 June 1870 the toll bridge opened, and pedestrians paid a halfpenny to cross; it is still known locally as the Halfpenny Bridge. It was closed to traffic in 1974, and in 1980 its centre span was replaced by a narrower footbridge. In 2014–15 it was widened again to carry the trams of Nottingham Express Transit, and it is now a tram, pedestrian and cycle bridge.","source":{"title":"Wilford Toll Bridge","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilford_Toll_Bridge","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1870","whenSrc":{"title":"Wilford Toll Bridge","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilford_Toll_Bridge","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Wilford Toll Bridge","see":[["Wilford Suspension Bridge","65157"],["Clifton Bridge","65666"]]}],"sum":"Wilford Toll Bridge, locally referred to as the 'Halfpenny Bridge', is a tram, pedestrian and cycle bridge in Nottingham, England. It crosses the River Trent between the Meadows and Wilford. It originally opened as a toll bridge for general traffic in 1870, but was closed when declared unsafe in 1974.","sumSrc":{"title":"Wilford Toll Bridge","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilford_Toll_Bridge"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/wilford.html"}],"xy":[-1.15451,52.93772]},"69732":{"h":6.5,"base":5,"osm":"way/466925985","kind":"bridge","sum":"NET Line 2 is a bridge.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.15463,52.93751]},"69733":{"where":"Robin Hood Way","h":8,"osm":"way/230766395","kind":"residential","council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.1551,52.93902]},"69734":{"where":"Briar Court","h":8,"osm":"way/230766392","kind":"residential","council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.15504,52.93909]},"69735":{"h":10,"osm":"way/61738805","kind":"public","sum":"Briar Court Communal Room is a community centre on Briar Court.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.15531,52.93896]},"69736":{"where":"Briar Court","h":8,"osm":"way/230766408","kind":"residential","council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.15543,52.93903]},"69737":{"where":"Briar Court","h":8,"osm":"way/61738790","kind":"residential","council":"Nottingham City Co